From the perspective of multiport network theory, the information-bearing symbols are physically realized as current excitations across the antenna ports in the frequency domain $(f)$. Thus, the current vector over all $N$ transmit antennas can be expressed as

    \begin{equation}
        {{ \textbf{i} }}_{\mathrm{T}} (f) \in \operatorname{\mathbb{C}}^{N \times 1} \triangleq {\bf F}_{\mathrm{EM}}(f) {\bf F}_{\mathrm{RF}}(f) {\bf F}_{\mathrm{BB}}(f) {\bf s}(f),
    \end{equation}

    which is driven by the source current vector

    \begin{equation}
        {{ \textbf{i} }}_{\mathrm{S}}(f) \in \operatorname{\mathbb{C}}^{A \times 1} \triangleq {\bf F}_{\mathrm{RF}}(f) {\bf F}_{\mathrm{BB}}(f) {\bf s}(f).
    \end{equation}

    This formulation bridges the signal processing domain with the antenna domain, where antenna port currents generate radiated fields to convey the symbol vector ${\bf s}(f)$. The EM-layer precoder, ${\bf F}_{\mathrm{EM}}$, captures the mutual coupling effects and impedance matching conditions inherent in the array. For notational simplicity, we henceforth omit the explicit frequency dependence, with all frequency-dependent quantities evaluated at the carrier frequency $f_c$.
